# +Point, -Surface
# -Template
# +Spelling, +Height, -Successors
# +Spelling, +Target, -Goal
#   * <module> The set-free series: depth-first search on a DAG with many paths
#
# A family of the set-free series on an algorithm shape among the
# attractors: a noinline search found as recursive descent may be replaced
# by breadth-first search at compile time. A point is a closed pure lambda program, no set in it,
# whose answer is a Scott Boolean: whether depth-first search, the
# attractor `depth_first` of corpora/contest_attractors.pl, finds a target
# node in a ladder.
#
# THE LADDER of height n has the nodes 0 ... n, unary numerals; node k < n
# has two edges, both to k + 1, and node n none. So there are 2^n paths from
# 0 to n, and depth-first search without a visited set pops a node once per
# path that reaches it: 2^(n+1) - 1 pops when the target is not in the
# ladder, against n + 1 nodes for a search that remembers the nodes it has
# visited. The successors and the goal are written with the attractors
# natural_equal, successor, cons and nil.
#
# THE POINTS:
# - REACHABLE (target n): the first path reaches n, so every strategy is
#   quick; a check of the encoding;
# - UNREACHABLE (target n + 1): depth-first search pops 2^(n+1) - 1 nodes;
# - each in a DIRECT and a RESPELLED twin, as in contest_set_free_arithmetic.pl;
# - NEAR MISS, a proved trap for replacement by shape: the search that tests
#   the goal only at a leaf, a node with no successor (leaves_only_template/1),
#   with an interior target. It is no respelling of `depth_first`; a setting
#   that answers it as reachability answers true where the program's normal
#   form is false.
#
# TARGET ALGORITHMS, the specific known algorithms the problem targets, each
# with its prior work:
# - depth-first search that marks the nodes it visits runs in time linear in
#   the nodes and edges: R. Tarjan, Depth-First Search and Linear Graph
#   Algorithms, SIAM J. Comput. 1972, doi:10.1137/0201010;
# - tabled evaluation, which answers a repeated subgoal from its table
#   instead of evaluating it again: W. Chen, D. S. Warren, Tabled Evaluation
#   with Delaying for General Logic Programs, J. ACM 1996,
#   doi:10.1145/227595.227597;
# - search strategies (depth-first, breadth-first, bounded) as
#   implementations of one algebraic specification, so that one may be
#   replaced by another: J. M. Spivey, Algebras for Combinatorial Search,
#   J. Funct. Program. 2009, doi:10.1017/S0956796809007321;
# - recognising the attractor after compression: equality saturation (Tate
#   et al., POPL 2009, doi:10.1145/1480881.1480915) and call restoration by
#   higher-order patterns (D. Miller, J. Logic Comput. 1991,
#   doi:10.1093/logcom/1.4.497).
#
# THE SNAPSHOT is the Boolean of the reference: target =< n for depth_first,
# target = n for the leaves-only search. PROOF. The program is pure, so
# S(P) = {N} for its normal form N. The search ends:
# each step pops the top node k and pushes its successors, two nodes k + 1
# for k < n and none for n, so the multiset of the values n - k on the stack
# decreases in the multiset order, which is well founded. depth_first
# answers true exactly when some popped node satisfies the goal, and the
# nodes popped are all of 0 ... n; the leaves-only search tests the goal only
# at n. natural_equal on canonical numerals reduces to the Boolean of
# equality (the prelude's definition, by induction on the first numeral), so
# the program reduces to the Scott Boolean of the reference, a normal form.
# The near miss with target 3 < n reduces to false, and reachability's
# answer, true, is another normal form, so a setting answering true on it
# answers outside S(P).
# LIMITS: the reduction family's, 10 s, 256 MB, 256 MB.

#   ladder_point(?Point, ?Search, ?Spelling, ?Height, ?Target)
#
# The search is a constructor of set_free_search/search, depth_first or
# leaves_only; the spelling is a constructor of schema/spelling, direct or
# respelled.
# Each other text is from_cbor of a string literal, a Scott list of bytes,
# imported from corpora/library/text/cbor. Each number is from_cbor of an
# integer literal, a Scott integer, imported from corpora/library/integer/cbor.
# FIXME: the imports project "corpora" https://github.com/Atry/private-overlay/issues/1923
# off the variable gezi, the name of the project root's own
# directory; it breaks if that directory is renamed.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "list")
λ nil cons _ _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "text") "cbor")
λ text_from_cbor _ _ _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. text_from_cbor λx. x)
λ text
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "integer") "cbor")
λ integer_from_cbor _
. (λtuple. tuple λ. integer_from_cbor λx. x)
λ integer
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "schema") "spelling")
λ direct respelled
. omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "set_free_search") "search")
λ depth_first leaves_only
. export
λ
. cons
  ( λ _ladder_point
    . _ladder_point (text "set_free_ladder_small_reachable_direct") depth_first direct (integer 6) λ. integer 6
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _ladder_point
    . _ladder_point (text "set_free_ladder_small_reachable_respelled") depth_first respelled (integer 6) λ. integer 6
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _ladder_point
    . _ladder_point (text "set_free_ladder_small_unreachable_direct") depth_first direct (integer 6) λ. integer 7
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _ladder_point
    . _ladder_point (text "set_free_ladder_small_unreachable_respelled") depth_first respelled (integer 6) λ. integer 7
  )
λ
. cons
  ( λ _ladder_point
    . _ladder_point (text "set_free_ladder_small_near_miss") leaves_only direct (integer 6) λ. integer 3
  ) nil
